TITLE: ELK removes measured label collisions without losing semantic edges

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[EVIDENCE]
UID: EV-SDOC-BOARD-ELK-NEIGHBORHOOD-SMOKE
TITLE: ELK removes measured label collisions without losing semantic edges
DEPTH: verified
AUTHORED_BY: llm
SOURCE: Independent Chromium 152 headless DevTools probes at 1440x900 against the live scribe daemon, plus a synthetic pre-layout cap probe; 2026-09-03
STATEMENT: >>>
Against the live canon, independent headless Chromium rendered
SLICE-CHECKPOINT-WIRING at one, two, and three hops as respectively 13, 48, and
48 cards with 22, 110, and 110 semantic relations. The two- and three-hop views
therefore exercised the deterministic 48-node nearest-neighborhood cap. The
post-ELK coalescer produced 74, 420, and 420 real orthogonal segments with 8,
35, and 35 labels.

The reported Crosses route from the focal card to MECH-PLAN-REFS-CHECK ended at
screen x=839.743652 while the card began at x=839.743408 at fit, a 0.000244
pixel overlap rather than a gap. Near one-to-one scale the overlap was 0.001221
pixels, and pixel inspection found no canvas-colored column between the route
and card. The added bridge spans ELK's one-unit invisible junction only where
the same role's adjacent route pieces share semantic relations; the observed
bridge retained all six contributing Crosses relations.

At every depth, geometric comparison found zero exact same-role overlaps and
zero collinear same-role overlaps whose lane coordinates differed by one layout
unit or less. Bounding-box and stroke sampling found zero label-label,
label-node, and label-to-unrelated-edge intersections. Every path remained
solid, inspection and dimming semantics remained consistent across visible
nodes, and the page reported no runtime, console, or network errors.
<<<
